course: adult-life
lesson: 45
title: The royal family in the news: a queen's death, a coronation, titles lost and illnesses announced
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When a famous person is ill, people online rush to guess. In early 2024 the Princess of Wales was away after planned surgery, and the internet filled with «Where is Kate?» theories — wild, cruel, and none of them true. Then she told the world herself, in a video, that she had cancer and was having chemotherapy.
explain: That is the clearest lesson about health gossip: someone's illness is theirs to share. Know what was officially announced, and stop there. Guessing beyond it is not clever; it can hurt a family with young children who can read the comments.
explain: Scene: in Sydney in March 2024, Mum puts her phone down quietly. «Kate's done a video. She's got cancer. And all those people making jokes about her.» Her big sister had laughed at a «Where's Kate» meme the week before, and now she feels awful.
explain: Royal news also has words to watch. «It was reported that» means the news came from reliable reporting; «a royal source says» means a nameless person you cannot check; «I saw on TikTok» can mean anything at all, including a silly lie like «the King has been replaced by a double».
explain: With serious matters, like accusations against a member of the family, say what was officially announced, plainly, and leave the rest to the courts.
fact: In January 2022 the Palace announced that Prince Andrew's army titles and charities went back to the Queen, because of a serious court case about him in America.
fact: It was reported in October 2025 that the King was taking away Andrew's titles altogether, so he is now known as Andrew Mountbatten Windsor.
fact: In January 2024 Kensington Palace announced that the Princess of Wales had had planned surgery on her tummy and would be away until after Easter; the online theories about her absence were rumours, and none were true.
fact: In March 2024 photo agencies withdrew a Mother's Day family photo because it had been edited, and the Princess apologised.
fact: On 22 March 2024 the Princess of Wales announced in a video that she had cancer and was having chemotherapy, strong medicine to stop it coming back, and asked for privacy.
fact: In September 2024 she announced she had finished chemotherapy, and in January 2025 it was reported that she said she was in remission, meaning there was no sign of the cancer for now.
fact: In February 2024 the Palace announced that the King had a form of cancer, found while he was in hospital for a smaller problem, and was having treatment; the type was not made public.
fact: It was reported that in May 2025 Prince Harry lost a court case about having police guards when he visits Britain, and said in a BBC interview he would like to make up with his family.
fact: After Charles became King, William and Catherine became Prince and Princess of Wales, Edward was made Duke of Edinburgh in 2023, and Harry's children Archie and Lilibet, as the King's grandchildren, can use the titles prince and princess.
fact: Camilla became Queen when Charles became King and was crowned with him in May 2023.
fact: The Queen's death certificate, published in September 2022, gave «old age» as the cause of death.
fact: «A royal source says» is a nameless claim that can be right, wrong or made up; it is not the same as the person saying it.
fact: When two stories contradict each other and there is no proof, the honest answer is «we don't know».
fact: Prince Harry served about ten years in the Army, including two tours of Afghanistan, and founded the Invictus Games for wounded service people.
exercise: Dad, January 2022: «The Queen's taken away Andrew's military titles and charities.» What was announced? | They went back to the Queen, as the Palace said | He was sent to prison by the Queen herself for a few years at the Tower of London | He was made to leave the country for good | He gave them to his brother Edward
exercise: Mum, October 2025: «Andrew's lost his titles, not even Duke of York now.» How is he known now, as reported? | Andrew Mountbatten Windsor | Prince Andrew of Windsor | The Duke of Edinburgh | Lord Andrew of York
exercise: Big sister, March 2024: «Where's Kate?? Everyone online's got a theory!» What had the Palace actually announced? | She'd had planned surgery and would be away till after Easter | She had left the royal family for good and would not be coming back at all | She was on a long secret holiday abroad | Nothing at all, they kept it all secret
exercise: Auntie Jen: «That Mother's Day photo was photoshopped! The agencies pulled it!» What's the confirmed fact? | It had been edited and she apologised | It hid a big secret about the family | It was taken years before, not that week | It was made by a computer, not a camera
exercise: Mum, March 2024: «Kate's got cancer, she said so in a video.» What did she ask for? | Privacy for her family | A public holiday for the nation | Everyone to guess what it was | The newspapers to print the details
exercise: Nan, September 2024: «Kate's finished her chemo! Lovely little film.» Who shared the news? | The Princess herself | A royal source in the papers | A fan account on TikTok | Her old school friend
exercise: Grandad, February 2024: «The King's got cancer too. They didn't say what kind.» What did the Palace say about the type? | It was not made public | It was the same kind as Kate's | It was very mild and gone | It was printed in full in the papers
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Harry flew over for less than an hour! That's how bad it is!» What's another fair reading? | A quick trip across an ocean can also show love | It proves they hate each other now | It proves the King refused to see him at all on the trip | It means Harry is moving back for good
exercise: Dad: «Harry lost his court case about police protection.» What's the careful way to say it? | It was reported he lost the case in May 2025 | Harry was put in prison by the court | The King took Harry to court himself | Harry won the case and got his police guards back for good
exercise: Big sister: «William and Harry haven't said a word in years.» Where does this come from? | Unnamed sources; the brothers gave no details | A statement signed by both of the brothers together | A court case between the two brothers | The official royal family website
exercise: Nan: «William's Prince of Wales now, Kate's Princess of Wales, like Diana.» What's fact and what's opinion? | The titles are fact; «the next Diana» is comparison | Both the titles and the comparison are fact, checked by the palace | The titles are a rumour, not confirmed | Neither one has ever been announced
exercise: Auntie Jen: «Archie and Lilibet are prince and princess now.» Why? | As the King's grandchildren they can use the titles | They won the titles in a court case | The Queen gave them the titles as a present just before she died | Harry bought the titles for them
exercise: Mum: «Meghan's got a cooking show and a jam brand. Everybody's got an opinion.» What's the fair line? | You can dislike a show without hating the person | Everyone must like whatever she makes | Bad reviews prove she's a bad person who should stop | Nobody should be allowed to review it
exercise: Grandad: «Papers say a royal source says the King's FURIOUS!» What is a royal source? | A nameless person you can't check | The King speaking on the record | A statement from the Palace | A court record anyone can read
exercise: Big brother: «TikTok says the King's secretly dead and they use a double!» What's true? | He's been filmed at many events since; it's a lie | Nobody knows, it could be true | It's true, they did it with the Queen too, for years and years | The Palace admitted they use a double
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Camilla's Queen now. Diana would have…» What's wrong with «Diana would have…»? | It puts words in the mouth of someone who died | It's true, Diana said it on television | Diana is still alive and could answer the question herself | Nothing, everyone knows what she thought
exercise: Mum: «Louis pulled faces all through the Jubilee, cheeky monkey!» What's the kind way to enjoy it? | Laugh with a tired small boy, not at him for years | Call him naughty and out of control | Share memes of him every year forever, on his birthday | Say his parents should be ashamed
exercise: Nan: «Edward's Duke of Edinburgh now, like his dad.» When? | March 2023 | April 2021 | September 2022 | May 2018
test: Grandad: «Queen's death certificate said 'old age'. 96, she'd done her bit.» What should you do with other rumours about how she died? | Trust the official record, not the rumours | Share them all, because some of them might be true | Ask the family what really happened | Believe the ones with the most likes
test: Auntie Jen: «Meghan made Kate cry before the wedding — or was it the other way round?» What's the honest answer? | Two claims, no proof, so we don't know | It was definitely Meghan who cried | It was definitely Kate who cried | They both cried because of the dress
test: Mum: «The royals were all rushing up to Balmoral the day she died. Who was late, eh?» What's the decent thing? | Leave a grieving family alone, flight times aren't character | Work out who was late and tell everyone | Check all the flight times on the internet and post them online | Blame whoever arrived after she died
test: Nan: «Harry loved being a soldier. Two tours of Afghanistan.» Which is confirmed? | He served about ten years, with two Afghan tours | He left the army in disgrace after just one year of training | He never went to Afghanistan at all | He is still a serving soldier today
test: Big sister: «The royals are all fighting in Instagram comments lol!» What's really there? | Official accounts post photos and news, no comment fights | The King replies to Harry's posts every day with long messages | Kate and Meghan argue under every photo | William blocked Harry on all the apps
test: Your mate in March 2024: «Kate's been replaced by a lookalike, ha!» A week later she shares her cancer news. What's the lesson? | Wild guesses about someone's health can be cruel | The lookalike story turned out to be right | Jokes about famous people never matter | It's fine because she'll never see it
test: Grandad: «The King's had cancer — I reckon it's really bad, he's hiding it.» What's the right reply? | We know what was announced; guessing more isn't okay | He's right, they always hide the bad news from the public | The King must be dying, they said so | We should ask the hospital to tell us
test: Dad: «Andrew? There's all sorts on the internet about him.» How should you talk about it? | Say what was officially announced and stop there | Repeat the internet stuff, it's all public anyway, so it's fine | Make up a funny meme about him | Tell everyone your own guess about him
test: Auntie Bev: «Harry flew back the same day after the coronation, says it all!» What does a flight actually tell you? | Where he went, not what he felt | That he hates his whole family | That he was thrown out of the party | That Meghan told him to come home
test: Big brother: «The edited photo PROVES Kate was hiding something!» Is that right? | No, lots of photos are edited; that doesn't make rumours true | Yes, editing a photo always hides a secret | Yes, the agencies said she was hiding something serious about her health | No, the photo wasn't edited at all
test: Mum, January 2025: «It was reported Kate said she's in remission.» What does «it was reported» tell you? | The news came from reliable reporting of her words | It's a rumour that nobody has checked | It's a guess made by a fan account on TikTok | It's something Mum made up herself
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises information attributed to «a royal source»? | It is an anonymous claim which cannot be independently verified | It is a formal communication issued on behalf of the monarch | It is evidence admitted in proceedings before a court of law | It is a statement the person concerned has publicly confirmed
exam: Identify the principal reason for which speculation concerning the illness of a public figure ought to be avoided. | The details of a person's health are private save as she chooses to disclose them | Public figures are legally exempt from all forms of illness | Speculation is permitted only by registered medical practitioners | The illness of a public figure is invariably less serious than reported
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Princess of Wales in 2024. | She announced a cancer diagnosis and a course of chemotherapy, and later its completion | She was replaced at public events by a person of similar appearance and bearing for several months | She declined to make any statement whatsoever concerning her health | She relinquished her title in consequence of her illness
exam: It is given that two published accounts of a private disagreement contradict one another and no proof exists. Determine the appropriate conclusion. | That the truth of the matter is not known | That the earlier account must be correct | That the later account must be correct | That both accounts must be correct
